Wire `verimark_handshake_perform_async()` (Phase 10.3.c) into the
`verimark_open()` vfunc. The GMainLoop now stays unblocked during
the two USB round-trips of the TLS handshake (~30-50 ms : CH→SH,
flight2→server_resp).

Architecture :
- Sync prelude in open() : USB reset + 300 ms settle + claim_interface
  + init_sequence (2 ctrl transfers ~10 ms) + sub1 file load +
  optional bootstrap. Total ~50-100 ms, dominated by the intentional
  300 ms reset settle — not a GMainLoop concern.
- Heap-owned `VerimarkOpenCtx` with deep-copied `VerimarkPairingData`
  (SECRET, zeroed on free).
- Async handshake fired with the ctx as user_data. Callback
  `verimark_open_handshake_done` :
  - On HANDSHAKE_OK : set `session_active = TRUE`, wipe ctx, call
    `fpi_device_open_complete`.
  - On HANDSHAKE_ERR_SERVER_ALERT (and `!attempted_recovery`) : send
    plaintext close_notify (7 bytes sync — no keys needed per
    RFC 5246 §7.2.1) + drain, set `attempted_recovery = TRUE`, re-fire
    `verimark_handshake_perform_async`. The recovery write/drain
    stays sync : two tiny control transfers (~5 ms each) don't
    justify another async chain.
  - On any other failure : log, leave `session_active = FALSE`, call
    `open_complete` with NULL error (consistent with the sync code).

No fpi_ssm here : just a single async chain with at most one retry.
The retry-recovery loop is gated by `attempted_recovery` so we can't
infinitely re-fire on persistent TLS Alerts.

Caveat : the bootstrap path (factory-fresh pairing generation) is
left sync. It's a rare branch (only on first-time setup with no sub1
file) and the keypair generation + signing dominates wall time anyway.

Builds clean (zero warnings) against libfprint 1.94.10 / libgusb
0.4.9 on NixOS. 11 standalone TDD suites stay green.

Runtime validation pending : nixos-rebuild + fprintd-list + fprintd-
verify should all succeed with the same UX as before, just with
the GMainLoop responsive during open() instead of blocking ~50 ms.

AI-assisted.
